<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="arith-reading" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Addition and Multiplication of Real Numbers</title>
  
  <p>
    At this point, we have finally gotten to defining what the elements of the set of real numbers are.  To summarize our process, we began with the set <m>A</m> whose elements are Cauchy sequences of rational numbers.  We then defined a relation <m>\sim</m> on <m>A</m> given by <m>\{ x_n \} \sim \{ y_n \}</m> precisely when <m>\{ x_n-y_n \}</m> converges to 0.  We proved that this relation is an equivalence relation, which means that we can split up the set <m>A</m> into equivalence classes.  Each equivalence class is a real number, so we define <m>\mathbb{R}</m> to be the set of all equivalence classes of <m>A</m> under the equivalence relation <m>\sim</m>.
  </p>

<example>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let us figure out what the real number <m>\pi</m> is.  Recall that <m>\pi</m> has a decimal representation <m>3.1415926...</m>.  This decimal representation is actually a Cauchy sequence in disguise.  If we take the sequence <m>\{ 3,3.1,3.14,3.141,3.1415,3.14159,3.141592, \ldots \}</m>, this is a sequence that converges to <m>\pi</m>.  In the way we defined our real numbers, the above Cauchy sequence <em>represents</em> the number <m>\pi</m>.  So what is <m>\pi</m>?  It is the equivalence class that contains this representative Cauchy sequence.  In symbols, <m>\pi = [ \{ 3,3.1,3.14,3.141,3.1415,3.14159,3.141592, \cdots \} ]</m>.  We can do the same thing for every real number.
    </p>
  </statement>
</example>

<p>
  Now that we know how to define real numbers, we move on to showing how to add them together.  As a model, we look at addition of rational numbers.  Consider adding <m>\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{3}</m>.  Are you annoyed at my sloppiness?  You should be.  When I write <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>, what I really mean is the rational number represented by the fraction <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>, and similarly for <m>\frac{1}{3}</m>.  So if I am being a good mathematician, I should write this as <m>[\frac{1}{2}]+[\frac{1}{3}]</m>, where the square brackets indicate the equivalence classes of the fractions (e.g. <m>[\frac{1}{2}]</m> is the set of all fractions that are equivalent to <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>).  When we define addition, we use our understanding of how addition of fractions works.  If we are sloppy, we get <m>\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{3} = \frac{3+2}{6}=\frac{5}{6}</m>.   If we are not sloppy, we get <m>[\frac{1}{2}]+[\frac{1}{3}] =[\frac{5}{6}]</m>, which is the set of all fractions equivalent to <m>\frac{5}{6}</m>.  In general, we get <m>[\frac{a}{b}]+[\frac{c}{d}] = [\frac{ad+bc}{bd}]</m>.
</p>

<p>
  Let's try this for real numbers.  As a reminder, we can assume that we already know how to add and multiply rational numbers.  If we want to add two real numbers, we first look for rational Cauchy sequences that represent them.  Suppose the representative Cauchy sequences are <m>\{ a_n \} = \{ a_1,a_2,a_3, \ldots \}</m> and <m>\{ b_n \} = \{ b_1,b_2,b_3, \ldots \}</m>.  What Cauchy sequence should represent the sum of those two numbers?  If we start by being sloppy as we were with fractions, it makes sense to define <m>\{ a_n \} + \{ b_n \} = \{ a_n+b_n \} = \{ a_1+b_1,a_2+b_2,a_3+b_3, \ldots \}</m>.  If we are not sloppy, we get <m>[\{ a_n \}] + [\{ b_n \}] = [\{ a_n+b_n \}]</m>.
</p>

<p>
  The above definition of addition makes sense intuitively.  If <m>\{a_n\}</m> converges to <m>a</m>, and if <m>\{b_n\}</m> converges to <m>b</m>, then <m>\{a_n+b_n\}</m> converges to <m>a+b</m>.  However, it makes sense to ask if the definition has any logical contradictions.  In other words, can we manipulate the rules of addition to get two different answers to the same addition computation?  If so, that would make our addition definition not well defined.
</p>

<p>
  Believe it or not, we have this same potential issue when we add fractions.  We computed <m>[\frac{1}{2}]+[\frac{1}{3}] = [\frac{3+2}{6}]</m>, but that is not the only way we could have done that computation.  We could have noted that <m>\frac{1}{2} \sim \frac{2}{4}</m> and <m>\frac{1}{3} \sim \frac{4}{12}</m>, so we could have done the same computation as <m>[\frac{2}{4}]+[\frac{4}{12}] = [\frac{2 \cdot 12+4 \cdot 4}{4 \cdot 12}] = [\frac{40}{48}]</m>.  The answer should be <m>[\frac{5}{6}]</m>, as we computed before.  Is it?  We would have to check if <m>\frac{5}{6}</m> is equivalent to <m>\frac{40}{48}</m>.  It happens that they are equivalent, but to be careful, we would have to prove that we never get any inconsistencies in our computations.
</p>

<p>
  Thus, we have to check for inconsistencies with real number addition.  So what does an inconsistency look like?  Let's say that we are computing <m>[\{ a_n \}]+[\{b_n\}]</m>.  We have already explained that we can compute this to get <m>[\{ a_n \}]+[\{b_n\}]=[\{a_n+b_n\}]</m>.   How could we do this same computation according to the rules we set and get a potentially different answer?  Remember that each real number can be represented by more than one Cauchy sequence.  So there could be another Cauchy sequence that represents <m>[\{a_n\}]</m>.  Suppose <m>\{a_n'\}</m> is such a sequence.  Then <m>[\{a_n\}]=[\{a_n'\}]</m>, which means that <m>\{a_n\} \sim \{a_n'\}</m>.  Similarly, we can have a Cauchy sequence <m>\{b_n'\}</m> with <m>\{b_n'\} \sim \{b_n\}</m>, which means <m>[\{b_n\}] = [\{b_n'\}]</m>.  That gives us a different way of computing <m>[\{ a_n \}]+[\{b_n\}]</m>.  We get
  <md>[\{ a_n \}]+[\{b_n\}]=[\{a_n'\}]+[\{b_n'\}] = [\{a_n'+b_n'\}]</md>
</p>

<p>
  So we computed <m>[\{ a_n \}]+[\{b_n\}]</m> twice to get <m>[\{a_n+b_n\}]</m> and <m>[\{a_n'+b_n'\}]</m>.  Did we get two different answers?  We hope not, but how can we check?  We want to know whether or not <m>[\{a_n+b_n\}]=[\{a_n'+b_n'\}]</m>.  The only way to check this is to see whether or not <m>[\{a_n+b_n\}] \sim [\{a_n'+b_n'\}]</m>.  So let's check.  We have <m>\{a_n\} \sim \{a_n'\}</m>, so <m>\{a_n-a_n'\}</m> converges. to 0.  Similarly, <m>\{b_n-b_n'\}</m> converges to 0.  Now we need to look at <m>\{(a_n+b_n) - (a_n'+b_n')\}</m>.  Here is what it converges to.
  <md>
    <mrow>\lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \{(a_n+b_n) - (a_n'+b_n')\} \amp =  \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \{a_n+b_n - a_n'-b_n'\}</mrow>
    <mrow>\amp = \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \{ (a_n-a_n')+(b_n-b_n')\}</mrow>
    <mrow>\amp = \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \{ a_n-a_n' \} + \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \{b_n-b_n'\}</mrow>
    <mrow>\amp = 0+0 = 0</mrow>
  </md>
</p>

<p>
  That means <m>[\{a_n+b_n\}] \sim [\{a_n'+b_n'\}]</m>, which implies that there are no inconsistencies in addition.  In other words, addition is well defined.
</p>

<p>
  We do have one last issue to resolve for addition.  We have defined <m>[\{a_n\}]+[\{b_n\}] = [\{a_n+b_n\}]</m>.  Is our result a real number?  To check this, we need to determine that <m>\{a_n+b_n\}</m> is a Cauchy sequence.  You get to check that as part of one of your portfolio problems.  I will let you take care of multiplication as well.
</p>

</section>